# EuroDNS Provider

Manages DNS records of zones hosted at [EuroDNS](https://www.eurodns.com/) through the
[EuroDNS User API](https://docapi.eurodns.com/).

One provider, two modes, the same resources:

| Mode | Configuration | Credentials held by | Guarantees |
|------|---------------|---------------------|------------|
| Direct | `app_id` / `api_key` | Terraform | Records outside the configuration are written back unchanged; per-process write serialization only |
| Gate | `gate` block | [eurodns-gate](https://github.com/KamorionLabs/eurodns-gate) | OIDC identity, RBAC per zone/name/type, the gate only changes RRsets it created or adopted, zone-level concurrency control |

The two modes are mutually exclusive. Switching mode only changes the provider block: the state keeps
the same resource ids. RRsets created in direct mode are foreign to the gate and must be adopted
(by importing them in gate mode) before the gate lets Terraform change them.

## Rate limit

EuroDNS allows 10 requests per 10 seconds per source IP and bans offenders progressively. The provider
reads each zone once per run, serializes writes per zone and paces requests (`request_interval`,
default `1.1s`). Every write rewrites the whole zone (the API has no per-record update), so it costs a
read and a save.

## Record data

Values are sent to EuroDNS as written:

- Names must be fully qualified with a trailing dot (CNAME, MX, NS, SRV, PTR and APEX targets). EuroDNS
  treats a name without it as relative to the zone (`target.example.net` becomes
  `target.example.net.example.com.`), so the provider refuses such values at plan time.
- TXT values are stored raw, quotes included: write `v=spf1 -all`, not `"v=spf1 -all"`. A quoted value
  produces a warning.
- The SOA record and the apex NS records are locked by EuroDNS (the SOA serial changes on every write)
  and cannot be managed or imported.

